  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 1252 AM MDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nThe most active day of the period for thunderstorms will be \nMonday as the next disturbance embedded within the southwesterly\nmonsoon moisture feed moves across the area during the \nafternoon and evening hours. Precipitable waters increase to \n150% of normal along the Continental Divide. Meanwhile, \nsoutheasterly surface winds across the plains will bring a \nreturn of dew points in the 50s, boosting CAPEs to a little over\n1000 J/kg across eastern portions of the southeast plains in\nsome of the higher res models. This may boost storm strengths \nand precipitation efficiencies across the southeast mountains \nand plains as thunderstorms develop over the mountains during \nthe afternoon and spread eastward into the southeast plains \nthrough the evening and overnight periods. There are some \ndifferences in the models with available low level moisture and \nresultant storm coverage across the southeast mountains and \nplains, with GFS on the drier side of the spectrum. Best chance\nfor heavy rains will still be out west along the Continental \nDivide where Willow Creek burn scar may be the most vulnerable \nflash flood risk. Aspen Acres carries a bit more uncertainty \nwhere probabilities of QPF over Flash Flood thresholds is less \nthan 20% in the latest runs. Will need to monitor this closely \nas the conceptually the risk could be higher should the low \nlevel moisture return be as robust as some of the high res \nmodels suggest. Flash Flood Watches may become necessary for \nif models hone in towards a wetter solution. For now axis of \nheavier precipitation appears to stay across western and \nnorthern Colorado. \n\nA drying and warming trend returns for Tuesday through Thursday with \nmainly isolated thunderstorms possible during the afternoons and \nevenings.  Temperatures rebound back into the mid to upper 90s by \nlate week.  \n\nAnother batch of monsoon moisture spreads northward across CO late \nweek into next weekend in southwesterly flow aloft.  This should \nbring another increase in thunderstorm probabilities across the \nmountains beginning Friday. Low level moisture remains rather \nlack luster across the plains which should keep thunderstorms \nmore isolated and higher based with gusty winds, and localized \nlight to moderate rainfall possible. Temperatures will remain \nabove normal. -KT",
